The second term of eq. (2.17) gives rise to an infinite tower of interaction vertices on the worldline, all of which have a single graviton leg but any number of deflection legs, including none. The source of this tower can be identified by considering the Fourier transform,

$$ h_{\mu\nu}(x(\tau))=\int_{k}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k\cdot x(\tau)}h_{\mu\nu}(-k), $$

where the expansion of the exponential after inserting the inertial expansion produces

$$ \displaystyle h_{\mu\nu}(x(\tau)) \displaystyle=\int_{k}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\tau k\cdot v}\Bigg[\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{i}^{j}(k\cdot z(\tau))^{j}}{j!}\Bigg]h_{\mu\nu}(-k) \displaystyle=\int_{k}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\tau k\cdot v}\Bigg[\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{i}^{j}}{j!}\prod_{l=1}^{j}\int_{\omega_{l}}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\omega_{l}\tau}(k\cdot z(-\omega_{l}))\Bigg]h_{\mu\nu}(-k), $$

giving rise to, after performing the integration over $\tau$ ,

$$ \displaystyle\kappa S_{\text{wl}}[h,x] \displaystyle=-\kappa M\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{i}^{j}}{j!}\int_{k,\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{j}}\hat{\delta}(k\cdot v+\omega_{1\cdots j})h_{\mu\nu}(-k)\prod_{l=1}^{j}z^{\rho_{l}}(-\omega_{l}) \displaystyle\times\Bigg[\frac{1}{2}\prod_{n=1}^{j}k_{\rho_{n}}v^{\mu}v^{\nu}+\sum_{n=1}^{j}\prod_{m\neq n}^{j}\omega_{n}k_{\rho_{m}}\!v^{(\mu}\delta^{\nu)}_{\rho_{n}}+\sum_{n<m}^{j}\prod_{s\neq n,m}^{j}\omega_{n}\omega_{m}k_{\rho_{s}}\delta^{(\mu}_{\rho_{n}}\delta^{\nu)}_{\rho_{m}}\Bigg]\,, $$

with

$$ \omega_{1\cdots j}=\sum_{i=1}^{j}\omega_{i}. $$

As it turns out, for this paper, we require only the pieces that are zeroth and first order in the deflection. The zeroth-order piece,

$$ \kappa S_{\text{wl}}[h,x]\big|_{z^{0}}=-\frac{\kappa M}{2}\int_{k}\hat{\delta}(k\cdot v)v^{\mu}v^{\nu}h_{\mu\nu}(-k), $$

is related to the energy-momentum tensor of the Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric, which will be discussed in the next section. It gives rise to a Feynman rule where the worldline, represented by a faint dotted line, sources a graviton,

