We will use these rules in the calculation of the Compton amplitude at first post-Minkowskian order in section 3 and second post-Minkowskian order in section 4. First, we consider how to derive Feynman rules in a curved space expansion.

Note that in quantum field theories, defined in the bulk of spacetime, all Feynman rules are accompanied by momentum-conserving delta functions coming from the ubiquitous presence of $\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x$ in the action. However, since the deflection is defined only on the worldline, its Feynman rules contain merely energy-conserving delta functions. Therefore, in contrast to tree-level computations in the bulk, loop-like integrals will appear in the tree-level diagrammatic expansion of our theory due to the leftover momenta which are not constrained by momentum conservation.

### 2.2 Curved space expansion

We will now consider an expansion where the background field is a non-trivial position-dependent metric. By inserting this expansion into the Einstein-Hilbert action, gauge-fixed using the background-covariant harmonic gauge, and disregarding total derivatives, we find the terms linear and quadratic in $h$ ,

$$ \displaystyle S_{\text{EH}}[\bar{g}+\kappa h]\big|_{h^{1}} \displaystyle=\frac{2}{\kappa}\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}\bar{G}_{\mu\nu}h^{\mu\nu}, \displaystyle S_{\text{EH}}[\bar{g}+\kappa h]\big|_{h^{2}}+S^{\text{curv}}_{\text{gf}}[h] \displaystyle=\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}\Bigg[\frac{1}{4}\bar{R}(2h^{\mu\nu}h_{\mu\nu}-h^{2}) \displaystyle\hskip 50.00008pt+\bar{R}_{\mu\nu}(h^{\mu\nu}h-h^{\mu\lambda}h^{{\mathchoice{\makebox[4.74307pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[4.74307pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[2.85834pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[2.04167pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\nu}}}_{{{\lambda}\mathchoice{\makebox[4.00928pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[4.00928pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[2.42052pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[1.72893pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}})-\bar{R}_{\rho\nu\sigma\mu}h^{\mu\nu}h^{\rho\sigma} \displaystyle\hskip 50.00008pt+\frac{1}{2}\bar{\nabla}_{\rho}h_{\mu\nu}\bar{\nabla}^{\rho}h^{\mu\nu}-\frac{1}{4}\bar{\nabla}_{\mu}h\bar{\nabla}^{\mu}h\Bigg]. $$

Here, we use $h\equiv\bar{g}^{\mu\nu}h_{\mu\nu}$ and raise and lower indices with the background metric, $\bar{g}_{\mu\nu}$ . When we discuss the worldline action below, we will demonstrate how the contribution from eq. (2.28) cancels when we choose $\bar{g}_{\mu\nu}$ to be the Schwarzschild-Tangherlini solution. For now, we concentrate on the quadratic term, splitting the action into a flat and an interacting part,

$$ S_{\text{G}}^{\text{free}}[h]=\eqref{eq:quad-eh-term}\big|_{\bar{g}\to\eta},\qquad S_{\text{G}}^{\text{int}}[h]=\eqref{eq:quad-eh-term}-S_{\text{G}}^{\text{free}}[h]. $$

$S_{\text{G}}^{\text{free}}[h]$ is identical to the quadratic piece of the Einstein-Hilbert action in the weak-field expansion and gives rise to the de Donder propagator of eq. (2.11). Expanding the covariant derivatives in the interaction action leaves us with

$$ \displaystyle S_{\text{G}}^{\text{int}}[h]=\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\Big[\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{0}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\gamma\delta}h_{\mu_{1}\nu_{1},\gamma}h_{\mu_{2}\nu_{2},\delta}+\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{1}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\delta}h_{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}}h_{\mu_{2}\nu_{2},\delta}+\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{2}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}h_{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}}h_{\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}\Big], $$

where the subscript on the tensors, $\bar{\Phi}$ , reflects the number of derivatives of the metric contained in them. Using11 1 Notice that $T^{{{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}}}_{{{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}\,\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}}$ is defined entirely in terms of Kronecker deltas and is thus not dependent on the metric.

$$ T^{{{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}}}_{{{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}\,\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}}\equiv I^{{{\mu_{1}(\mu_{2}}\mathchoice{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[9.8333pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[8.30948pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}}_{{\mathchoice{\makebox[18.74965pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[18.74965pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[12.31052pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[10.07895pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}}}}I^{{{\nu_{2})\nu_{1}}\mathchoice{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[9.8333pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[8.30948pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}}_{{\mathchoice{\makebox[17.04356pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[17.04356pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[11.24658pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[9.31897pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}}-\frac{1}{2}I^{{{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}}\mathchoice{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[9.8333pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[8.30948pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}}_{{\mathchoice{\makebox[14.77159pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[14.77159pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[9.873pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[8.33784pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}}}}I^{{{\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}\mathchoice{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[14.68292pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[9.8333pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[8.30948pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}}_{{\mathchoice{\makebox[14.77159pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[14.77159pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[9.873pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[8.33784pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}}, $$

we can spell out

$$ \displaystyle\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{0}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\gamma\delta} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{8}(\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}\bar{g}^{\gamma\delta}\bar{g}^{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}}\bar{g}^{\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}-\eta^{\gamma\delta}\eta^{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}}\eta^{\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}})T^{{{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}}}_{{{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}\,\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}}, \displaystyle\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{1}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\delta} \displaystyle=-\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}\bar{g}^{\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}T^{{{\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\delta(\nu_{1}}}}_{{{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}\,\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}}\bar{\Gamma}^{\mu_{1})\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}}, \displaystyle\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{2}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}} \displaystyle=\operatorname{sym}P_{2}\frac{1}{8}\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}\Big(4\bar{g}^{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}}\bar{g}^{\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}\bar{\Gamma}^{{{\mu_{1}}\mathchoice{\makebox[8.94359pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[8.94359pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[5.3952pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[3.85373pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}}_{{\mathchoice{\makebox[7.81232pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[7.81232pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[5.20248pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[4.35892pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\gamma\lambda}}}\bar{\Gamma}^{{{\mu_{2}}\mathchoice{\makebox[3.63474pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[3.63474pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[2.17776pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[1.55554pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\lambda}}}_{{\mathchoice{\makebox[7.81232pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[7.81232pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[5.20248pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[4.35892pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\delta}\mathchoice{\makebox[4.74307pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[4.74307pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[2.85834pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[2.04167pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}}T^{{{\nu_{1}\gamma\,\nu_{2}\delta}}}_{{{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}\,\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}}+\bar{R}\bar{g}^{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}}\bar{g}^{\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}T^{{{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}}}_{{{\rho_{1}\sigma_{1}\,\rho_{2}\sigma_{2}}}} \displaystyle\hskip 45.00006pt+16\bar{g}^{\mu_{1}[\nu_{1}}\bar{R}^{\mu_{2}]\nu_{2}}-8\bar{R}^{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\nu_{1}\nu_{2}}\Big), $$

where $P_{2}$ denotes symmetrization in the index pairs, $(\mu_{1},\nu_{1})$ and $(\mu_{2},\nu_{2})$ , and sym denotes symmetrization between $\mu_{i}$ and $\nu_{i}$ , and with eq. (2.31) in hand, we can finally Fourier transform the gravitons and make the crossing symmetry of the vertex explicit, yielding

$$ \displaystyle S_{\text{G}}^{\text{int}}[h] \displaystyle=\int_{k_{1},k_{2}}h_{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}}(-k_{1})h_{\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}(-k_{2})\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(k_{1}+k_{2})\cdot x} \displaystyle\times\bigg(-\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{0}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\gamma\delta}k_{1\gamma}k_{2\delta}+\frac{\mathrm{i}}{2}\Big[\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{1}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\delta}k_{1\delta}+\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{1}]}^{\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\delta}k_{2\delta}\Big]+\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{2}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}\bigg). $$

