where

$$ \displaystyle\bar{V}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}(k_{1},k_{2})=\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x} \displaystyle\times\big(-2\mathrm{i}\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{0}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\gamma\delta}k_{1\gamma}k_{2\delta}+\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{1}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\delta}k_{1\delta}+\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{1}]}^{\mu_{2}\nu_{2}\,\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\delta}k_{2\delta}+2\mathrm{i}\bar{\Phi}_{[\partial^{2}]}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}\big). $$

Note that the gray thick “graviton” line attached to the massive body worldline is only introduced to denote all-order-in- $\kappa^{2}$ interactions with the background that are incorporated into this two-point vertex.22 2 The contributions from the background metric map to interactions involving potential gravitons in a flat space expansion. We emphasize that no properties of the Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric were used in the derivation of this formula. Therefore, the vertex will take this form for any background geometry.

Let us now specialize to the Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric in $d$ dimensions,

$$ \bar{g}_{\mu\nu}(x)=\Big(1+\frac{\Lambda_{d}}{4\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{d-3}}\Big)^{\frac{4}{d-3}}\,\eta_{\perp\mu\nu}+\frac{\big(1-\frac{\Lambda_{d}}{4\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{d-3}}\big)^{2}}{\big(1+\frac{\Lambda_{d}}{4\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{d-3}}\big)^{2}}\,\eta_{\parallel\mu\nu}. $$

Many choices of coordinate gauge are available; here we choose the isotropic one as was done in [98, 99]. In the interest of maintaining manifest Lorentz covariance, we have written the metric in an arbitrary asymptotic inertial frame by defining the restricted metrics,

$$ \displaystyle\eta_{\parallel\mu\nu} \displaystyle\equiv v_{\mu}v_{\nu}, \displaystyle\eta_{\perp\mu\nu} \displaystyle\equiv\eta_{\mu\nu}-v_{\mu}v_{\nu}, $$

which are projection operators onto the one-dimensional subspace collinear with the black hole velocity, $v^{\mu}$ , satisfying $v^{2}=1$ , and the $(d-1)$ -dimensional subspace perpendicular to said velocity, respectively. When used on a vector such as $x^{\mu}$ , we will adopt the self-explanatory notation,

$$ x_{\perp}^{\mu}\equiv\eta^{{{\mu}}}_{{{\perp\nu}}}x^{\nu}, $$

as seen in eq. (2.37) in conjunction with $\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert\equiv\sqrt{\lvert x_{\perp}^{2}\rvert}$ . We also define the dimensionally dependent scale,

$$ \Lambda_{d}\equiv\frac{\kappa^{2}M}{2(d-2)\Omega_{d-2}},\qquad\text{with}\qquad\Omega_{d-2}\equiv\frac{2\pi^{\frac{d-1}{2}}}{\Gamma(\frac{d-1}{2})}, $$

where $\Omega_{d-2}$ is the surface area of the $(d-2)$ -dimensional unit sphere. We note that the advantage of isotropic coordinates is twofold: Firstly, they can be easily written in terms of $\eta_{\mu\nu}$ and $v_{\mu}$ as in eq. (2.37), making tensor manipulations simple. Secondly, when expanded, the powers of $G$ and $\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert$ are correlated, so that an expression of order $G^{n}$ is an $(n-1)$ -loop integral.

In this work, we wish to verify that we obtain the same result using the curved and flat space Feynman rules. Hence, we can expand the background metric in eq. (2.37),

$$ \bar{g}_{\mu\nu}(x)=\eta_{\mu\nu}-\frac{\Lambda_{d}}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{d-3}}\Big(\eta_{\parallel\mu\nu}-\frac{\eta_{\perp\mu\nu}}{d-3}\Big)+\cdots, $$

and insert it into eq. (2.36), after which the Fourier transform may be evaluated order by order in $\kappa^{2}$ . Putting all of this together, the momentum space vertex factor can be expanded as

$$ \bar{V}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}(k_{1},k_{2})=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\bar{V}_{(i)}^{\mu_{1}\nu_{1}\,\mu_{2}\nu_{2}}(k_{1},k_{2}), $$

