To derive Feynman rules involving the worldline, it is helpful to decompose the background metric into the flat Minkowski metric and a remainder that accounts for the curvature,

$$ \bar{g}_{\mu\nu}(x)=\eta_{\mu\nu}+\bar{\gamma}_{\mu\nu}(x). $$

Then, as in the case of the weak-field expansion, the linearity of the worldline action in the metric results in

$$ S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{g}+\kappa h,x]=S_{\text{wl}}[\eta,x]+\kappa S_{\text{wl}}[h,x]+S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]. $$

The first two terms in this expanded action are precisely those found in eq. (2.17). The additional term, $S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]$ , which includes the non-trivial, curved portion of the metric evaluated on the worldline, describes the self-force experienced by the body represented by the worldline. One might be concerned about the divergence that arises when $\bar{\gamma}_{\mu\nu}(x)$ is evaluated at the location of the black hole; however, as is shown in appendix C and also outlined in [100, 98], this part of the action is scaleless and vanishes in dimensional regularization. This leaves us with the same action as in the weak-field expansion,

$$ S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{g}+\kappa h,x]=S_{\text{wl}}[\eta+\kappa h,x], $$

implying that the curved space Feynman rules for the worldline are identical to the ones derived from the weak-field expansion. The simplest of these rules is the source rule, eq. (2.24), which we can rewrite in terms of the energy-momentum tensor of the background,

$$ \displaystyle\bar{T}^{\mu\nu}(x) \displaystyle=\frac{-2}{\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}}\frac{\delta S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{g},v\tau]}{\delta\bar{g}_{\mu\nu}(x)} \displaystyle=\frac{M}{\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}}\int\mathrm{d}\tau\,\delta^{(d)}(x-v\tau)v^{\mu}v^{\nu}. $$

To wit,

$$ \kappa S_{\text{wl}}[h,x]\big|_{z^{0}}=-\frac{\kappa}{2}\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}\bar{T}^{\mu\nu}(x)h_{\mu\nu}(x). $$

When this term is added to the linear part of the Einstein-Hilbert action from eq. (2.28), we obtain Einstein’s equation for the background,

$$ S_{\text{EH}}[\bar{g}+\kappa h]\big|_{h^{1}}+\kappa S_{\text{wl}}[h,x]\big|_{z^{0}}=\frac{2}{\kappa}\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\sqrt{\lvert\bar{g}\rvert}\Big[\bar{G}^{\mu\nu}(x)-\frac{\kappa^{2}}{4}\bar{T}^{\mu\nu}(x)\Big]h_{\mu\nu}(x), $$

which vanishes identically, as it should, when $\bar{g}_{\mu\nu}$ solves Einstein’s equation (the Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric being the solution in this case). Therefore, as the linear-in- $h$ terms of the action vanish, the curved space Feynman rules contain no vertex where the heavy body sources a graviton, killing this class of diagrams. The effect of these types of diagrams in flat space is reconfigured and encoded in the curved space Einstein-Hilbert graviton vertices through the presence of a non-flat metric. Diagrammatically speaking,

