Here, we have integrated out the deflection energy using the delta function from one of the vertices and fixed the energy flowing through the worldline to $\omega=p_{1}\cdot v$ , the energy of the graviton. The resulting expression is equivalent to the contraction of external graviton polarization tensors with the recoil vertex in eq. (2.55). Summing the contributions from both diagrams, we note that all dependence on $d$ cancels automatically and the result is independent of the dimension. If we factorize the polarization tensors,

$$ \varepsilon_{i\mu\nu}\equiv\varepsilon_{i\mu}\varepsilon_{i\nu}, $$

we can recover the familiar gauge-invariant double copy form [113] of the classical first post-Minkowskian order Compton amplitude,

$$ \mathrm{i}\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\mathcal{M}^{\text{1PM}}(p_{1},p_{2})=-\mathrm{i}\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\frac{\kappa^{2}M}{2}\frac{(v\cdot f_{1}\cdot f_{2}\cdot v)^{2}}{q^{2}\omega^{2}}, $$

expressed in terms of the momentum space field strength tensor of the incoming and outgoing gravitons,

$$ f_{i\mu\nu}\equiv p_{i\mu}\varepsilon_{i\nu}-p_{i\nu}\varepsilon_{i\mu}, $$

using the notation, $(V\cdot f_{i})^{\mu}=V^{\nu}f^{{\mathchoice{\makebox[6.83856pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[6.83856pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[4.10863pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[2.93472pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}{\mu}}}_{{{i\nu}\mathchoice{\makebox[4.86232pt][c]{$\displaystyle$}}{\makebox[4.86232pt][c]{$\textstyle$}}{\makebox[2.95248pt][c]{$\scriptstyle$}}{\makebox[2.10892pt][c]{$\scriptscriptstyle$}}}}$ . As noted in [98], the normalization of the amplitude in eq. (3.4) differs by a factor of $2M$ from the conventional result, $\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}^{\text{1PM}}(p_{1},p_{2})$ . This arises purely from different conventions as seen from

$$ \mathrm{i}\hat{\delta}(2q\cdot Mv)\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}^{\text{1PM}}(p_{1},p_{2})=\mathrm{i}\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\mathcal{M}^{\text{1PM}}(p_{1},p_{2}). $$

