{}{}{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}=\mathrm{i}\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\kappa^{4}M^{2}\int_{\ell}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell\cdot v)N_{\text{v}}(\varepsilon_{1},\varepsilon_{2},\ell)}{\ell^{2}(p_{1}+\ell)^{2}(q-\ell)^{2}}. $$

All the diagrams have unit symmetry factor. Upon adding contributions from all diagrams, we find an integrand such that every integral that appears is a member of the integral family,

$$ K_{\nu_{1},\nu_{2},\nu_{3},\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}}=\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}\int_{\ell}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell\cdot v)(\varepsilon_{1}\cdot\ell)^{\lambda_{1}}(\varepsilon_{2}\cdot\ell)^{\lambda_{2}}}{[\ell^{2}]^{\nu_{1}}[(p_{1}+\ell)^{2}]^{\nu_{2}}[(q-\ell)^{2}]^{\nu_{3}}}. $$

Using integration-by-parts identities, implemented using LiteRed [123, 124], we can express every integral in this family as a linear combination of the three master integrals [125],

$$ \mathcal{K}_{1}=K_{0,1,0,0,0},\qquad\mathcal{K}_{2}=K_{1,0,1,0,0},\qquad\mathcal{K}_{3}=K_{1,1,1,0,0}, $$

and express the total second-order post-Minkowskian amplitude in this basis of integrals. As mentioned above, we also perform the computation in the weak-field expansion where the contributing diagrams and their respective symmetry factors are given in eq. (2.57). Naturally, the same integral family appears in this computation, and we find, reassuringly, complete agreement between the expansion coefficients calculated using curved space diagrams or flat space diagrams.

### 4.2 Result

We have computed the amplitude and verified its gauge-invariance in $d$ dimensions, which is ensured by highly non-trivial cancellations occurring between the diagrams. The $d$ -dimensional result is complicated, so here we report only the expansion around $d=4-2\epsilon$ . In appendix B, we show the $\epsilon$ -expansion of the master integrals to be

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{1} \displaystyle=-\frac{\mathrm{i}\omega}{4\pi}\Bigg[1+\epsilon\bigg(\mathrm{i}\pi+2-\log\frac{4\omega^{2}}{\mu^{2}}\bigg)\Bigg]+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2}), \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{2} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{8\lvert q\rvert}+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon), \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{3} \displaystyle=-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{8\pi q^{2}\omega}\Bigg[\frac{1}{\epsilon}-\log\frac{-q^{2}}{\mu^{2}}\Bigg]+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon), $$

where we have included terms up to the order needed to determine the finite part of the amplitude. Expanded in the basis of master integrals, the amplitude takes the form,

$$ \mathrm{i}\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\mathcal{M}^{\text{2PM}}(p_{1},p_{2})=\mathrm{i}\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\kappa^{4}M^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{3}c_{i}\mathcal{K}_{i}, $$

where the gauge-invariant master integral coefficients, $c_{i}$ , are polynomials in dot products of the graviton momenta, polarizations, and the worldline velocity, with every term naturally restricted to be linear in each polarization tensor. Products of the gauge-invariant combinations,

$$ \displaystyle\mathsf{F}_{1} \displaystyle=\frac{v\cdot f_{1}\cdot f_{2}\cdot v}{\omega^{2}}, \displaystyle\mathsf{F}_{2} \displaystyle=\frac{(v\cdot f_{1}\cdot p_{2})(v\cdot f_{2}\cdot p_{1})}{\omega^{4}}, $$

span this space, where $f_{1}$ and $f_{2}$ were defined in section 3. We can therefore express the coefficients in the gauge-invariant basis, $\{\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2},\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2},\mathsf{F}_{1}\mathsf{F}_{2}\}$ [126]. The explicit expressions for the master integral coefficients can be found in table 1, where we use the ratio, $y=-q^{2}/4\omega^{2}$ , defined in appendix B. In appendix D, we verify that the coefficients match those obtained from heavy-mass effective field theory.

| $c_{1}^{(-1)}$ | $\frac{1}{48\epsilon}\bigg[\frac{5y-6}{2(y-1)^{2}}\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}-\frac{2+y}{8(y-1)^{4}}\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}+\frac{y-2}{(y-1)^{3}}\mathsf{F}_{1}\mathsf{F}_{2}\bigg]$ |
|---|---|
| $c_{1}^{(0)}$ | $\frac{1}{288}\bigg[\frac{4y-3}{(y-1)^{2}}\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}+\frac{4+17y}{8(y-1)^{4}}\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}+\frac{7-2y}{(y-1)^{3}}\mathsf{F}_{1}\mathsf{F}_{2}\bigg]$ |
| $c_{2}^{(0)}$ | $\frac{\omega^{2}}{384}\bigg[\frac{45+15y-45y^{2}+y^{3}}{(y-1)^{2}}\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}+\frac{9+39y-y^{2}+y^{3}}{4(y-1)^{4}}\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}+\frac{27+27y-23y^{2}+y^{3}}{(y-1)^{3}}\mathsf{F}_{1}\mathsf{F}_{2}\bigg]$ |
