## Appendix A Fourier transform

We review the Fourier transform needed to compute the two-point vertex from eq. (2.36) in the curved space expansion. The Fourier transform contains an integration along the velocity of the black hole, $v^{\mu}$ , which trivially evaluates to a delta function,

$$ \displaystyle\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x}\frac{1}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{n}} \displaystyle=\int\mathrm{d}^{d-1}x_{\perp}\mathrm{d}x_{\parallel}\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x}\frac{1}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{n}} \displaystyle=\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\int\mathrm{d}^{d-1}x_{\perp}\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x_{\perp}}\frac{1}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{n}}. $$

Then, using $\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{n}=(\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{2})^{\frac{n}{2}}$ and introducing a Schwinger parameter, we get

$$ \displaystyle\int\mathrm{d}^{d-1}x_{\perp}\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x_{\perp}}\frac{1}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{n}} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\frac{n}{2})}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}\alpha\,\alpha^{\frac{n}{2}-1}\int\mathrm{d}^{d-1}x_{\perp}\,\mathrm{e}^{-\alpha\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{2}+\mathrm{i}q\cdot x_{\perp}} \displaystyle=\frac{\pi^{\frac{d-1}{2}}}{\Gamma(\frac{n}{2})}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}\alpha\,\alpha^{\frac{n-d-1}{2}}\exp\frac{q^{2}}{4\alpha} \displaystyle=\frac{(4\pi)^{\frac{d-1}{2}}\Gamma(\frac{d-1-n}{2})}{2^{n}\Gamma(\frac{n}{2})}\frac{1}{(-q^{2})^{\frac{d-1-n}{2}}}. $$

We can recover the result used in the main text by substituting $n=d-3$ . To wit,

$$ \displaystyle\int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x}\frac{1}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{d-3}} \displaystyle=\frac{4\pi^{\frac{d-1}{2}}}{\Gamma(\frac{d-3}{2})}\frac{\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)}{-q^{2}} \displaystyle=\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\frac{(d-3)\Omega_{d-2}}{-q^{2}}, $$

where we used $\Gamma(n+1)=n\Gamma(n)$ and $\Omega_{d-2}$ was defined in eq. (2.40).

For terms at order $\kappa^{2n}$ , we need

$$ \int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x}\frac{\Lambda_{d}}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{d-3}}=\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\frac{\Lambda_{d}(d-3)\Omega_{d-2}}{-q^{2}}. $$

Notice that the prefactor combines nicely to give

$$ \Lambda_{d}(d-3)\Omega_{d-2}=\frac{\kappa^{2}M}{2}\frac{d-3}{d-2}. $$

Using the fact that a product in position space is a convolution in momentum space,

$$ \int\mathrm{d}^{d}x\,\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}q\cdot x}\frac{\Lambda_{d}^{n}}{\lvert x_{\perp}\rvert^{n(d-3)}}=\hat{\delta}(q\cdot v)\frac{(\kappa^{2}M)^{n}}{2^{n}}\frac{(d-3)^{n}}{(d-2)^{n}}\int_{\ell_{1},\ldots,\ell_{n-1}}\frac{(-1)^{n}\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\cdots\hat{\delta}(\ell_{n-1}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2}\cdots\ell_{n-1}^{2}(q-\ell_{1\cdots(n-1)})^{2}}, $$

where $\ell_{1\cdots(n-1)}=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\ell_{i}$ . These types of integrals can be evaluated exactly for any $n$ .

## Appendix B Master integrals

In the second post-Minkowskian order computation, we encounter the one-loop integral family,

$$ K_{\nu_{1},\nu_{2},\nu_{3},\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}}=\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}\int\frac{\mathrm{d}^{d}\ell}{(2\pi)^{d}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell\cdot v)(\varepsilon_{1}\cdot\ell)^{\lambda_{1}}(\varepsilon_{2}\cdot\ell)^{\lambda_{2}}}{[\ell^{2}]^{\nu_{1}}[(p_{1}+\ell)^{2}+\mathrm{i}0]^{\nu_{2}}[(q-\ell)^{2}]^{\nu_{3}}}, $$

where we omit the $\mathrm{i}0$ prescription on the first and last propagators, as the delta function forces these momenta to be potential modes which are never on-shell. We find that three master integrals span this family,

$$ \mathcal{K}_{1}=K_{0,1,0,0,0},\qquad\mathcal{K}_{2}=K_{1,0,1,0,0},\qquad\mathcal{K}_{3}=K_{1,1,1,0,0}, $$

the cut bubble, cut triangle, and cut box, respectively (see figure 1). The first two are simple to evaluate. After integrating out the delta function, the cut bubble is

$$ \mathcal{K}_{1}=\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}\int\frac{\mathrm{d}^{d-1}\ell_{\perp}}{(2\pi)^{d-1}}\frac{1}{(\ell_{\perp}+p_{1\perp})^{2}+\omega^{2}+\mathrm{i}0}. $$

Shifting the loop momentum and using the primitive,

$$ \int\frac{\mathrm{d}^{n}k}{(2\pi)^{n}}\frac{1}{k^{2}+\Delta}=\frac{\Gamma(1-\frac{n}{2})}{(4\pi)^{\frac{n}{2}}}\Delta^{\frac{n}{2}-1}, $$

continued to $\Delta<0$ and with $n=d-1$ , we obtain

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{1} \displaystyle=-\frac{\Gamma(\epsilon-\frac{1}{2})}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}}\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}(-\omega^{2}-\mathrm{i}0)^{\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon} \displaystyle=-\frac{\mathrm{i}\omega}{4\pi}\Bigg[1+\epsilon\bigg(\mathrm{i}\pi+2-\log\frac{4\omega^{2}}{\mu^{2}}\bigg)\Bigg]+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon). $$

The cut triangle is, upon integrating out the delta function, a standard integral,

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{2} \displaystyle=\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}\int\frac{\mathrm{d}^{d-1}\ell_{\perp}}{(2\pi)^{d-1}}\frac{1}{\ell_{\perp}^{2}(q_{\perp}-\ell_{\perp})^{2}} \displaystyle=\frac{\Gamma(\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon)^{2}\Gamma(\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon)}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}\Gamma(1-2\epsilon)}\frac{\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}}{(-q^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon}} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{8\lvert q\rvert}+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon), $$

where we used $q_{\perp}=q$ . Finally, we come to the cut box, which, after integrating out the delta function, is

*Figure 1: The cut bubble $\mathcal{K}_{1}$ , the cut triangle $\mathcal{K}_{2}$ , and the cut box $\mathcal{K}_{3}$ . The red line signifies the propagator that is cut.*

$$ \mathcal{K}_{3}=\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}\int\frac{\mathrm{d}^{d-1}\ell_{\perp}}{(2\pi)^{d-1}}\frac{1}{\ell_{\perp}^{2}[(p_{1\perp}+\ell_{\perp})^{2}+\omega^{2}+\mathrm{i}0](q_{\perp}-\ell_{\perp})^{2}}. $$

We find it convenient to evaluate it with the method of differential equations [145, 146, 147, 148, 149]. Naïvely, the integral can depend independently on the energy, $\omega$ and, by Lorentz invariance, the squared momentum transfer, $q_{\perp}^{2}$ . However, by extracting the mass dimension from all the momenta,

$$ P\to\frac{P}{\omega},\quad\text{where}\quad P=\ell_{\perp},p_{1\perp},q_{\perp}, $$

$\omega$ appears only as a prefactor, implying that the integral depends only on the ratio, $y$ , of the two kinematic invariants,

$$ \mathcal{K}_{3}(\omega,q^{2})=\omega^{-3-2\epsilon}\tilde{\mu}^{2\epsilon}\mathsf{K}_{3}(y),\qquad y=\frac{-q^{2}}{4\omega^{2}}. $$

We can see from

$$ q_{\perp}^{2}=p_{1\perp}^{2}+p_{2\perp}^{2}-2p_{1\perp}\cdot p_{2\perp}=-2\omega^{2}(1-\cos\theta), $$

where $\theta$ is the angle between $p_{1\perp}$ and $p_{2\perp}$ , that the physical region is $y\in[0,1]$ , with $y\to 0$ being the forward-scattering limit. The differential equation is derived using LiteRed,

$$ \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}y}\mathsf{K}_{3}(y)=\frac{1}{y(y-1)}\bigg(\frac{1-2\epsilon}{8}\mathsf{K}_{1}(y)-\frac{\epsilon}{2}\mathsf{K}_{2}(y)-(y-1-\epsilon)\mathsf{K}_{3}(y)\bigg), $$

where $\mathsf{K}_{1}$ and $\mathsf{K}_{2}$ are the cut bubble and cut triangle with their $\omega$ dependence properly extracted using eq. (B.9). This differential equation has a solution in terms of hypergeometric functions,

$$ \displaystyle\mathsf{K}_{3}(y) \displaystyle=(1-y)^{\epsilon} \displaystyle\times\Bigg(y^{-1-\epsilon}C(\epsilon)+A_{\mathsf{K}_{1}}(\epsilon)\,{}_{2}F_{1}\Big[\begin{gathered}\scriptstyle 1{+}\epsilon,\,1{+}\epsilon,\\[-5.0pt]
\scriptstyle 2+\epsilon\end{gathered};y\Big]+y^{-\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon}A_{\mathsf{K}_{2}}(\epsilon)\,{}_{2}F_{1}\Big[\begin{gathered}\scriptstyle\frac{1}{2},\,1{+}\epsilon,\\[-5.0pt]
\scriptstyle\frac{3}{2}\end{gathered};y\Big]\Bigg), $$

where $C(\epsilon)$ is an integration constant, and

$$ A_{\mathsf{K}_{1}}(\epsilon)=\mathrm{i}(-1)^{-\epsilon}\frac{\Gamma(\epsilon-\frac{1}{2})}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}},\qquad A_{\mathsf{K}_{2}}(\epsilon)=\frac{1}{2^{1+2\epsilon}}\frac{\Gamma(\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon)^{2}\Gamma(\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon)}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}\Gamma(1-2\epsilon)}. $$

To determine the boundary constant, notice that the asymptotic expansion of the integral in the forward-scattering limit is

$$ \mathsf{K}_{3}(y)\sim\frac{C(\epsilon)}{y^{1+\epsilon}}\quad\text{as}\quad y\to 0. $$

We can determine this asymptotic expansion with the method of regions. We follow the approach proposed in [150, 151] (see also appendix A in [26]), which allows us to perform the expansion at the level of Feynman parameters. Using Feynman parameters,

$$ \displaystyle\mathsf{K}_{3}(y) \displaystyle=-\frac{\Gamma(\frac{3}{2}+\epsilon)}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}}\int_{\alpha_{i}\geq 0}\mathrm{d}^{3}\alpha\,\delta\Big(1-\sum_{i\in S}\alpha_{i}\Big)\frac{(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{3})^{2\epsilon}}{(4y\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}-\alpha_{2}^{2}-\mathrm{i}0)^{\frac{3}{2}+\epsilon}} \displaystyle=-\frac{\Gamma(\frac{3}{2}+\epsilon)}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}}\int_{\alpha_{i}\geq 0}\mathrm{d}^{2}\alpha\,\frac{(1+\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{3})^{2\epsilon}}{(4y\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}-1-\mathrm{i}0)^{\frac{3}{2}+\epsilon}}, $$

where we used the Cheng-Wu theorem to choose $S=\{2\}$ [152]. The method instructs us to find all variable substitutions, $\alpha_{i}\to y^{v_{i}}\alpha_{i}$ , that result in a non-scaleless integral with $y$ -homogeneous Symanzik polynomials after expanding the integrand in $y$ . We find that the only such substitution is $\alpha_{i}\to y^{-\frac{1}{2}}\alpha_{i}$ , which leads to an integral we can readily evaluate,

$$ \begin{array}[]{cc}\begin{aligned} \mathsf{K}_{3}(y)&\sim-\frac{1}{y^{1+\epsilon}}\frac{\Gamma(\frac{3}{2}+\epsilon)}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}}\int_{\alpha_{i}\geq 0}\mathrm{d}^{2}\alpha\,\frac{(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{3})^{2\epsilon}}{(4\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}-1-\mathrm{i}0)^{\frac{3}{2}+\epsilon}}\\
&=(-1-\mathrm{i}0)^{\frac{5}{2}-\epsilon}\frac{\pi}{4}\frac{\big(\mathrm{i}-\cot\pi\epsilon\big)\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon\big)\cos\pi\epsilon}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}}\frac{1}{y^{1+\epsilon}},\end{aligned}&\qquad\text{as}\quad y\to 0.\end{array} $$

Comparing this with eq. (B.15), we can identify $C(\epsilon)$ . Finally, we may determine the $\epsilon$ -expansion of the cut box to be

$$ \mathcal{K}_{3}=-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{8\pi q^{2}\omega}\Bigg[\frac{1}{\epsilon}-\log\frac{-q^{2}}{\mu^{2}}\Bigg]+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon). $$

## Appendix C Scaleless contributions to the worldline action

To prove the scaleless-ness of non-trivial contributions from the background metric evaluated at the position of the massive body, we start by manipulating $S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]$ analogously to $S_{\text{wl}}[h,x]$ in section 2.1. We first Fourier transform the curved part of the metric,

$$ \bar{\gamma}_{\mu\nu}(x(t))=\int_{\ell_{1}}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\ell_{1}\cdot x(\tau)}\bar{\gamma}_{\mu\nu}(-\ell_{1}). $$

Expanding the exponential in eq. (C.1) and inserting yields an expression identical to what we obtained before, i.e.,

$$ \displaystyle S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x] \displaystyle=-M\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{i}^{j}}{j!}\int_{\ell_{1},\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{j}}\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v+\omega_{1\cdots j})\bar{\gamma}_{\mu\nu}(-\ell_{1})\prod_{l=1}^{j}z^{\rho_{l}}(-\omega_{l}) \displaystyle\times\Bigg[\frac{1}{2}\prod_{n=1}^{j}k_{\rho_{n}}v^{\mu}v^{\nu}+\sum_{n=1}^{j}\prod_{m\neq n}^{j}\omega_{n}k_{\rho_{m}}\!v^{(\mu}\delta^{\nu)}_{\rho_{n}}+\sum_{n<m}^{j}\prod_{s\neq n,m}^{j}\omega_{n}\omega_{m}k_{\rho_{s}}\delta^{(\mu}_{\rho_{n}}\delta^{\nu)}_{\rho_{m}}\Bigg]. $$

For the $z^{j}$ order piece of this equation, we can say that

$$ S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]\big|_{z^{j}}\propto\int_{\ell_{1},\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{j}}\prod_{l=1}^{j}z^{\rho_{l}}(-\omega_{l})N^{\mu\nu}_{\rho_{1}\cdots\rho_{j}}[\ell_{1},\{\omega_{i}\}_{i=1..j}]\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v+\omega_{1\cdots j})\gamma_{\mu\nu}(-\ell_{1}), $$

where we collected the numerator factors in $N^{\mu\nu}_{\rho_{1}\cdots\rho_{j}}[\ell_{1},\{\omega_{i}\}_{i=1..j}]$ . As we showed above, $\bar{\gamma}_{\mu\nu}(-\ell_{1})$ is given at order $\kappa^{2n}$ by the cut triangle-type $(n-1)$ -loop integral in eq. (A.6). Thus, extracting the $\kappa^{2n}$ -order piece of the above, we have

$$ \displaystyle S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]\big|_{z^{j}\kappa^{2n}}\propto \displaystyle\int_{\ell_{1},\ldots,\ell_{n},\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{j}}\prod_{l=1}^{j}z^{\rho_{l}}(-\omega_{l})N^{\mu\nu}_{\rho_{1}\cdots\rho_{j}}[\ell_{1},\{\omega_{i}\}_{i=1..j}] \displaystyle\times\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v+\omega_{1\cdots j})\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)\cdots\hat{\delta}(\ell_{n}\cdot v)}{\ell_{2}^{2}\cdots\ell_{n}^{2}(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2\cdots n})^{2}}. $$

Using $\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)$ , and substituting $\ell_{1}\to\ell_{1}-\ell_{2\cdots n}$ , we are left with

$$ \displaystyle S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]\big|_{z^{j}\kappa^{2n}} \displaystyle\propto\int_{\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{j}}\prod_{l=1}^{j}z^{\rho_{l}}(-\omega_{l})\hat{\delta}(\omega_{1\cdots j}) \displaystyle\times\int_{\ell_{1},\ldots,\ell_{n}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)\cdots\hat{\delta}(\ell_{n}\cdot v)N^{\mu\nu}_{\rho_{1}\cdots\rho_{j}}[\ell_{1}-\ell_{2\cdots n},\{\omega_{i}\}_{i=1..j}]}{\ell_{1}^{2}\ell_{2}^{2}\cdots\ell_{n}^{2}}. $$

When expanded, each piece of the above factorizes into manifestly scaleless integrals. To see this, we notice by inspecting eq. (C.2) that each term in the numerator, $N^{\mu\nu}_{\rho_{1}\cdots\rho_{j}}$ , from the above expression will be proportional to products of the form,

$$ \prod_{i=1}^{\iota}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{2\cdots n})_{\rho_{s_{i}}}, $$

where $\iota=j-2,j-1,j$ corresponds to terms coming from the first, second, and third terms of eq. (C.2), and $s_{i}=1,\ldots,j$ . When expanded, a term in eq. (C.6) will, in general contain $1\leq R\leq\iota$ different loop momenta, meaning that it can be written as

$$ \prod_{i=1}^{R}\prod_{s_{i}}\ell_{r_{i}\rho_{s_{i}}}, $$

where $\rho_{s_{i}}$ denotes the indices that $\ell_{r_{i}}$ has in the term. With this knowledge, we can deduce that any term in $S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]\big|_{z^{j}\kappa^{2n}}$ will be proportional to a product of integrals of the form

$$ \Bigg[\int_{\ell}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell\cdot v)}{\ell^{2}}\Bigg]^{n-R}\prod_{i=1}^{R}\int_{\ell_{r_{i}}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{r_{i}}\cdot v)\prod_{s_{i}}\ell_{r_{i}\rho_{s_{i}}}}{\ell_{r_{i}}^{2}} $$

These integrals are all scaleless, so $S_{\text{wl}}[\bar{\gamma},x]$ vanishes in dimensional regularization. Diagrammatically, we can understand this fact as the vanishing of all diagrams where the worldline emits and absorbs a thick background “graviton”, e.g.,

