where the ellipsis denotes higher PM orders and where we have defined

$$ \displaystyle{\cal O}^{\alpha\mu\nu}(u_{j},p)\!=\!\frac{1}{2}((u_{j}^{\mu}\eta^{\nu\alpha}+u_{j}^{\nu}\eta^{\mu\alpha})(u_{j}p)-u_{j}^{\mu}u_{j}^{\nu}p^{\alpha})\,. $$

together with the frequency-domain trajectory, $\bar{x}^{\mu}_{i}(\omega)=\int d\tau\,e^{-i\omega\tau}\bar{x}_{i}^{\mu}(\tau)$ , and we have also explicitly expanded to subleading order. Case in point, we can trivially recast the trajectories in Eq. (9) into the form of perturbative Feynman diagrams. Concretely, using identities such as

$$ \displaystyle\textrm{arcsinh}\left(\frac{v\tau}{b}\right) \displaystyle=\frac{1}{\partial_{\tau}}\left(\frac{v}{(b^{2}+v^{2}\tau^{2})^{1/2}}\right)\,, $$

we can write the 1PM time-domain trajectory as

$$ \displaystyle\bar{x}_{1}^{\mu} \displaystyle=-\frac{r_{S}}{2v^{2}b^{2}}(2v^{2}+1)b^{\mu}(b^{2}+v^{2}\tau^{2})^{1/2} \displaystyle+\frac{r_{S}}{2v^{2}}\left(\sigma(2v^{2}-1)u_{H}^{\mu}+u_{L}^{\mu}\right)\frac{1}{\partial_{\tau}}\frac{1}{(b^{2}+v^{2}\tau^{2})^{1/2}}\,. $$

Powers of the spatial distance $(b^{2}+v^{2}\tau^{2})^{1/2}$ can be rewritten as simple fourier integrals. In the frequency domain, the trajectory then has a simple form

$$ \displaystyle\bar{x}_{1}^{\mu}(\omega) \displaystyle=\frac{i(2\pi)^{3}r_{S}}{v^{2}}\int\frac{d^{4}k}{(2\pi)^{4}}e^{-ik\bar{x}_{0}}\delta(\omega-u_{L}k)\delta(u_{H}k) \displaystyle\times\left(\frac{8(2v^{2}+1)\Pi^{\mu\nu}}{b^{2}}\frac{k_{\nu}}{(k^{2})^{3}}-\frac{(\sigma(2v^{2}-1)u_{H}^{\mu}+u_{L}^{\mu})}{k^{2}(u_{L}k)}\right), $$

where $\Pi^{\mu\nu}=\eta^{\mu\nu}-v^{-2}(\sigma u_{H}^{\mu}-u_{L}^{\mu})u_{L}^{\nu}-v^{-2}(\sigma u_{L}^{\mu}-u_{H}^{\mu})u_{H}^{\nu}$ projects onto directions orthogonal to the four-velocities. Note that factor of $\omega^{-2}$ which one would expect from perturbatively solving the geodesic equation has been reduced in power to $\omega^{-1}$ in one term, and entirely eliminated in the other.

